     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. The FDA label doesn't support the premise for it.\n\nThe current US metformin labeling gives these maximums:\n- **Immediate-release:** 2,550 mg/day, usually as 850 mg three times daily. Single doses are generally capped at 1,000 mg in the dosing guidance.\n- **Extended-release:** 2,000 mg/day.\n\n1,700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day, which is twice the labeled maximum. This patient has normal renal and hepatic function. That lowers the risk but doesn't make the dose safe. At this dose I'd expect more GI toxicity and a higher risk of lactic acidosis, with no added glycemic benefit.\n\nIf you have a source showing otherwise, such as a specific label section, please send it and I'll look at it again. This could also be a unit or transcription error. For example, 1,700 mg might have been meant as the total daily dose.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day)\n- Metformin 850 mg PO twice daily with meals (1,700 mg/day)\n- Another dose you specify within the labeled range\n\nIf this is a deliberate off-label order, it should go through pharmacy and your institution's override process rather than through me."
      }
     ],
